<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.6.3: Interpreting Negative Exponents in Exponential Decay</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01346</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.6.3: Interpreting Negative Exponents in Exponential Decay</md:title>
    <md:uuid>7e26d98d-8cc2-4f2e-8bd5-e041f868c355</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <para id="para-00001">A person took some medicine but does not remember how much. Concerned that she took too much, she has a blood test
  every hour for several hours.</para>
      <table class="os-raise-midsizetable" id="table-00001">
        <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
            <entry>
             <emphasis>\(t\), time (hours)</emphasis>
            </entry>
            <entry>
             <emphasis>\(m\), medicine (mg)</emphasis>
            </entry>
          </row>
        <row>
            <entry>
        –3
      </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
        –1
      </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
        0
      </entry>
            <entry>
        100
      </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
        1
      </entry>
            <entry>
        50
      </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
        2
      </entry>
            <entry>
        25
      </entry>
          </row>
        </tbody></tgroup></table>
      <para id="para-00002">Answer the following questions. Double click on mathematical expressions/equations to enlarge.
</para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">Time \(t\) is measured in hours since the blood test, and amount of medicine in her body, \(m\), is
        measured in milligrams. What is the decay factor? That is, what is \(b\) in an equation of the form
        \(m=a\cdot b^t\)?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00004"/>
          <para id="para-00005">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00006"> \(b=\frac12\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00007"> What is \(a\)?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00008"/>
          <para id="para-00009">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00010">\(a=100\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00011">Find the amounts of medicine in the patient’s body when \(t\) is –1.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00012"/>
          <para id="para-00013">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00014">When \(t\) is –1, \(m\) is 200.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00015">Find the amounts of medicine in the patient’s body when \(t\) is –3.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00016"/>
          <para id="para-00017">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00018">When \(t\) is –3, \(m\) is 800.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00019">What does \(t=0\) mean in this context?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00020"/>
          <para id="para-00021">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00022"> \(t=0\) means the time the doctor did the first blood test.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <exercise>
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00023">What does \(t=-3\) mean in this context?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00024"/>
          <para id="para-00025">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00026"> \(t=-3\) means 3 hours before the doctor did the first test.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00027">The medicine was taken when \(t\) is –5. Assuming the person did not have any of the medication in
        her body beforehand, how much medicine did the patient take?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00028"/>
          <para id="para-00029">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00030">3,200 mg. Each hour, this is cut in half. After being halved 5 times, it leaves 100 mg, the amount of medicine in
      the person’s body when \(t=0\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <example id="exam-00001">
        <exercise>
          <problem id="prob-00008">
            <para id="para-00031">Create a graph by plotting the points whose coordinates are in the table from question 1 above. Make sure to
        draw and label tick marks on the axes.</para>
            <para id="para-00032">
              <figure id="fig-00001">
                <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_08_06_01.svg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
            <para id="para-00033">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.
   </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00008">
            <para id="para-00034">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00035">
              <figure id="fig-00002">
                <media alt="">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_08_06_02.svg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem id="prob-00009">
          <para id="para-00036">Based on your graph, when do you think the patient will have: 500 mg of medicine remaining in her body</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00009">
          <para id="para-00037"/>
          <para id="para-00038">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00039">Between 2 and 3 hours before the patient first had her blood tested</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem id="prob-00010">
          <para id="para-00040">Based on your graph, when do you think the patient will have: No medicine remaining in her body</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00010">
          <para id="para-00041"/>
          <para id="para-00042">Compare your answer:
       Your answer may vary, but here are some samples:
   </para>
          <list id="list-00001">
            <item>Sometime after 10 hours. The amount of medicine falls below 0.1 mg after about 10 hours and might be too small
        to be detectable after that point.</item>
            <item> Eventually, all of the medicine will be out of the patient’s body (but the mathematical model for the
        amount of medicine does not reach 0).</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
      <example id="exam-00002">
        <exercise>
          <problem id="prob-00011">
            
            
          </problem>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <!--Start interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
      <section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00002">
        <title>Activity</title>
        <exercise id="a1f54a9b-cea1-45c5-ac43-2ac7329663a6" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00012">
            <para id="para-00043">Without evaluating them, describe each of the following quantities as close to 0, close to 1, or much larger
        than 1.</para>
            <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
            <para id="para-00044">\(\frac1{1-2^{-10}}\)
       </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00011">
            <para id="para-00045">Correct! The answer is: close to 1</para>
            <para id="para-00046">The correct answer is: close to 1</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_5ca31138-9e4f-4b4d-ad63-509dbdb6a8ef" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00013">
            <para id="para-00047">Without evaluating them, describe each of the following quantities as close to 0, close to 1, or much larger
        than 1.</para>
            <para id="para-00048">\(\frac{2^{10}}{2^{10}+1}\)
       </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00012">
            <para id="para-00049">Correct! The answer is: close to 1</para>
            <para id="para-00050">The correct answer is: close to 1</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="edc064ea-76ed-49fe-aa30-d41afe493426" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00014">
            <para id="para-00051">Without evaluating them, describe each of the following quantities as close to 0, close to 1, or much larger
        than 1.</para>
            <para id="para-00052">\(\frac{2^{-10}}{2^{10}+1}\)
       </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00013">
            <para id="para-00053">Correct! The answer is: close to 0</para>
            <para id="para-00054">The correct answer is: close to 0</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="a715a50b-aebc-4bb7-9e97-346d824a0243" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00015">
            <para id="para-00055">Without evaluating them, describe each of the following quantities as close to 0, close to 1, or much larger
        than 1.</para>
            <para id="para-00056">\(\frac{1-2^{-10}}{2^{10}}\)
       </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00014">
            <para id="para-00057">Correct! The answer is: close to 0</para>
            <para id="para-00058">The correct answer is: close to 0</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="fff56ade-4ac8-4bb1-95e8-015e427ddb17" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00016">
            <para id="para-00059">Without evaluating them, describe each of the following quantities as close to 0, close to 1, or much larger
        than 1.</para>
            <para id="para-00060">\(\frac{1+2^{10}}{2^{-10}}\)
       </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00015">
            <para id="para-00061">Correct! The answer is: much larger than 1</para>
            <para id="para-00062">The correct answer is: much larger than 1</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--End interaction-->
    </section>

  <note class="self-check"><para>A census of hemlock trees is taken in a national park. The rangers have found that only \(\frac45\) of the population
    has survived each year. The data are shown in the table. Find the number of hemlocks that were in the park 2 years
    before the census was taken.</para>
  
  <table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
    <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
        <entry><emphasis>\(y\), years since census</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>\(h\), number of hemlocks</emphasis></entry></row>
    <row>          
      <entry>
        –2
      </entry>
