        32,768
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody></tgroup></table>
  </note>

<section>
  <title>Additional Resources</title>
  
  <section><title>Interpreting Negative Exponents in Exponential Decay</title>
    <para>Let’s look at a problem involving an exponential decay equation and negative exponents.</para>
    <para>A scientist records the population of a bacterial population in a petri dish. The data are shown in the table.</para>
    
    <table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
      <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
          <entry><emphasis>\(d\), days since first test</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>\(b\), number of bacteria</emphasis></entry></row>
      <row>
          <entry>
           –3
          </entry>
          <entry> </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry>
           –1
          </entry>
          <entry> </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry>
            0
          </entry>
          <entry>
           40,000
          </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry>
            1
          </entry>
          <entry>
           20,000
          </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry>
          3
          </entry>
          <entry>
           5,000
          </entry>
        </row>
      </tbody></tgroup></table>
    
    <para>The number of days \(d\) since the first test corresponds with the number of bacteria, \(b\), estimated to be alive in the petri dish.</para>
    <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
      <item>
        What is the decay factor? That is, what is \(f\) in an equation of the form \(b=n\cdot f^d\)? What is the starting population, \(n\), on the first day of testing?
        
        Since the number of bacteria found on day 0 is 40,000, then \(n = 40,000\). Since after each day, the number of bacteria is cut in half, then the decay factor, \(f\), is \(\frac12\).
        
        The equation to represent this situation is \(b=40,000\cdot\left(\frac12\right)^d\).
     </item>
      
      <item>
        What do \(d=-1\) and \(d=-3\) mean in this context?
        
    
    
        Since the day the first test is taken is defined as \(d=0\), then days with a negative value occured before the first test. \(d=-1\) means 1 day before the scientist did the first test. \(d=-3\) means 3 days before the scientist did the first test.
    
     </item>
      
      <item>
        How many bacteria were present 1 day before the first test?
        
    
        Substitute –1 for \(d\) in the equation \(b=40,000\cdot\left(\frac12\right)^d\).
    
        \(b=40,000\cdot\left(\frac12\right)^d\)
        \(b=40,000\cdot\left(\frac12\right)^{-1}\)
        \(b=40,000\cdot 2\)
        \(b=80,000\)
        
        There were 80,000 bacteria in the petri dish 1 day before the test.
     </item>
      
      <item>
        Assume the peak of the bacterial population was 3 days before the first test. How many bacteria were present at the peak of the population?
    
    
    
        
        Substitute –3 for \(d\) in the equation \(b=40,000\cdot\left(\frac12\right)^d\).
        
        \(b=40,000\cdot\left(\frac12\right)^d\) 
        \(b=40,000\cdot\left(\frac12\right)^{-3}\) 
        \(b=40,000\cdot 8\) 
        \(b=320,000\)
        
        There were 320,000 bacteria in the petri dish 3 days before the test at the peak of the
        population.
     </item>
    </list>
    <note class="try look-at-me"/>
    <title>Try It: Interpreting Negative Exponents in Exponential Decay</title>
    <example><exercise>
      <problem>
        
        <para>An investor has tracked the value of a particular investment since 2016 that has depreciated over time. The data are shown in the table.</para>
       
        
       
        <table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
          <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
              <entry><emphasis>\(y\), years since 2016</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>\(v\), value of investment ($)</emphasis></entry></row>
          <row>
              <entry>
               –2
              </entry>
              <entry> </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>
              –1
              </entry>
              <entry> </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>
                0
              </entry>
              <entry>
                36,000
              </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>
               1
              </entry>
              <entry>
               12,000
              </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>
                2
              </entry>
              <entry>
                4,000
              </entry>
            </row>
          </tbody></tgroup></table>
        
        
        
        <para>
            What is the equation that represents this situation?
         </para>
        <para>
            What do \(y=-1\) and \(y=-2\) mean in this context?
         </para>
        <para>
            If the value of the investment was at its highest in 2014, what was the maximum value of the investment?
         </para>
        <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.
       </para>
      
      </problem><solution>
      <para>Compare your answers:</para>
      
      <para>Here is how to solve this exponential growth problem using negative exponents:</para>
      
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>
          The equation that represents this situation is \(v=36,000\cdot\left(\frac13\right)^y\). The value of the investment in year 0 is $36,000, and the decay factor is \(\frac13\).
       </item>
      </list>
      
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>
          \(y=-1\) means 1 year before 2016, which is 2015. \(y=-2\) means 2 years before 2016, which is 2014.
       </item>
      </list>
      
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>
          The maximum value of the investment was $324,000.
          
          \(v=36,000\cdot\left(\frac13\right)^y\) 
          \(v=36,000\cdot\left(\frac13\right)^{-2}\) 
          \(v=36,000\cdot 9\) 
          \(v=324,000\)
    
       </item>
      </list>
    
    </solution></exercise>
    </example></section>
</section>

